PTLib 2.12-beta10 (OPAL stack): compare the option spec pointer against NULL

`spec' is a const char *, and `spec == '\0'` compiled only because '\0' was a
null pointer constant before C++11: the statement tested whether the pointer was
null.  Since C++11 the literal is an integer there and the comparison is
ill-formed, so this translation unit does not build with any current compiler.

The repair states the null comparison the original expression actually performed
and therefore preserves the behaviour exactly.  It deliberately does NOT change
the test to `*spec == '\0'`, which is what the message "Empty [] clause" implies
was intended: this patch set exists to make the pinned source compile on a
current toolchain, not to fix upstream bugs, and changing the condition would
make PArgList reject option specifications it accepts today.

Applies to: 10503462abe31a6c288a39f6f894a5ceb367abbc (branch v2_12)
Upstream:   https://git.code.sf.net/p/opalvoip/ptlib

diff --git a/src/ptlib/common/osutils.cxx b/src/ptlib/common/osutils.cxx
index 7098c5f..fa18322 100644
--- a/src/ptlib/common/osutils.cxx
+++ b/src/ptlib/common/osutils.cxx
@@ -1401,7 +1401,7 @@ bool PArgList::Parse(const char * spec, PBoolean optionsBeforeParams)
           return false;
         opts.m_section = PString(spec, end-spec);
         spec = end+1;
-        if (InternalSpecificationError(spec == '\0', "Empty [] clause in specification."))
+        if (InternalSpecificationError(spec == NULL, "Empty [] clause in specification."))
           return false;
       }
 
